---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Example from '../../components/Example.astro';
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const sections=[{id:'installation',title:'Load and activate'},{id:'glass-example',title:'Inherited material'},{id:'coverage',title:'Supported surfaces'},{id:'glass-controls',title:'Controls and variants'},{id:'fallbacks',title:'Preferences and fallback'},{id:'scoped-glass',title:'Scoped use'}];
---
<DocsLayout title="Glass material" active="glass" sections={sections}>
<link rel="stylesheet" href={base+'reva/reva.glass.css'}/>
<p class="doc-kicker">THEMES / MATERIAL</p><h1>Glass material</h1>
<p class="doc-lead">An optional frosted treatment for supported surfaces. Use it with light, dark or system colours; it does not replace the theme or require JavaScript.</p>
<Example id="installation" title="Load and activate" description="Load the matching extension after the core. Loading the file alone does not activate glass." render={false} html={'<!doctype html>\n<html lang="en" data-theme="dark" data-material="glass">\n<head>\n  <meta charset="utf-8">\n  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">\n  <title>Glass page</title>\n  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/reva/reva.css">\n  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/reva/reva.glass.css">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <!-- Supported descendants inherit glass. -->\n</body>\n</html>'}/>
<Example id="glass-example" title="Glass with a solid exception" description="The parent supplies material. A nearer solid value stops it; a nested glass value can restart it." glass html={'<section class="stack" data-theme="dark" data-material="glass">\n  <article class="card">\n    <h3>A frosted surface</h3>\n    <p>Your content remains ordinary HTML.</p>\n    <button type="button">Continue</button>\n  </article>\n  <section data-material="solid">\n    <article class="card">\n      <h3>A solid exception</h3>\n      <p>This surface stays opaque.</p>\n    </article>\n  </section>\n</section>'}/>
<section id="coverage"><h2>What receives glass?</h2><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Glass component coverage" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Targets</th><th scope="col">Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Cards, fieldsets, disclosures, dialogs and popovers</th><td>Frosted surface backing and blur; default surface opacity is 70%.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Top navigation and its desktop panels</th><td>Frosted surfaces; neutral menu toggles preserve navigation styling.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Buttons, .button links and native action inputs</th><td>Stronger coloured backing; default action opacity is 90%.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Badges, alerts, pagination items, list items and choice-panel labels</th><td>Stronger backing with blur, following the parent material scope.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Text inputs, selects, textareas and switches</th><td>Keep their normal control backing. Glass does not make every HTML element translucent.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Icons, breadcrumbs, toolbars and layout wrappers</th><td>Keep their foreground/layout behaviour; they do not add a glass surface of their own.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></section>
<Example id="glass-controls" title="Button variants retain their API" description="Appearance is inherited. Shared semantic variants also reach glass actions; existing classes remain supported. Material solid is the local opt-out." glass html={'<div class="row" data-material="glass" data-accent="violet" data-shape="pill">\n  <button type="button">Continue</button>\n  <button type="button" class="secondary">Back</button>\n  <button type="button" class="outline">Outline</button>\n  <button type="button" data-variant="danger">Delete</button>\n  <button type="button" data-material="solid">Solid exception</button>\n</div>'}/>
<section id="fallbacks"><h2>Readable backing and preferences</h2><p>Default blur is 16px. Use <code>--re-glass-opacity</code> and <code>--re-glass-button-opacity</code> for percentage-based opacity overrides, and <code>--re-glass-filter</code> for the filter. Reduced opacity requires review against the actual backdrop; blur alone does not guarantee readable text.</p><p><code>data-contrast="more"</code> makes the material opaque. Supported reduced-transparency and increased-contrast preferences remove translucency; forced colours and print use their documented component fallbacks. Without backdrop filtering, the normal opaque core styles remain. Preference paint respects local material boundaries: a solid section stays outside the glass scope, even inside a glass ancestor. List items, pagination and choice labels remove blur for forced colours and print.</p><p>Shape, density, borders, depth, fill and theme retain their existing meanings. <code>data-fill="solid"</code> removes gradients; use <code>data-material="solid"</code> to remove glass. Animated edges remain opt-in decoration on buttons and cards.</p></section>
<Example id="scoped-glass" title="Matching scoped files" description="Use the scoped core and scoped glass extension together; put the material setting on the .reva boundary or inside it." render={false} html={'<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/reva/reva.scoped.css">\n<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/reva/reva.glass.scoped.css">\n<section class="reva" data-theme="dark" data-material="glass">\n  <article class="card">A scoped frosted surface</article>\n</section>'}/>
<p><a href={base+'preview/glass/light/'}>Light glass preview</a> · <a href={base+'preview/glass/dark/'}>Dark glass preview</a> · <a href={base+'preview/glass/auto/'}>System glass preview</a></p>
<p class="doc-note">Backdrop filtering can create stacking and compositing constraints. Avoid unnecessary layers of blur and review your actual images, contrast and device performance. The documentation is not a completed device-validation report.</p>
</DocsLayout>
